00:38:49.667 --> 00:38:52.147
So there was a very nice and fairly simple observation

00:38:52.147 --> 00:38:53.527
out of a group at Stanford.

00:38:54.187 --> 00:38:55.887
That they called large language monkeys.

00:38:56.257 --> 00:38:58.417
And so the idea is that if you take

00:38:58.417 --> 00:39:00.207
some problem, say, what is two plus

00:39:00.447 --> 00:39:02.567
two for the sake of example, and

00:39:02.567 --> 00:39:04.747
you feed it into a language model,

00:39:04.977 --> 00:39:07.296
right, instead of just asking the language model to produce

00:39:07.297 --> 00:39:09.097
a single correct completion,

00:39:09.317 --> 00:39:11.617
you could sample many times from this model.

00:39:11.857 --> 00:39:14.016
And maybe some of these samples are right, maybe some

00:39:14.016 --> 00:39:16.136
of these generations are wrong, But the

00:39:16.137 --> 00:39:18.296
point is is that as long as you have access to

00:39:18.296 --> 00:39:20.077
some kind of oracular verifier,

00:39:20.566 --> 00:39:22.647
right, maybe for math this is translate everything

00:39:22.647 --> 00:39:24.787
into lean, maybe for code this is see

00:39:24.787 --> 00:39:26.867
if something compiles. Right? But as long as you

00:39:26.867 --> 00:39:28.977
have access to this oracular verifier,

00:39:28.977 --> 00:39:31.397
then you can just verify each one of these generations

00:39:32.017 --> 00:39:34.062
exhaustively and see if one of them is

00:39:34.062 --> 00:39:36.266
correct. And obviously, What is two plus

00:39:36.266 --> 00:39:37.967
two is a fairly simple example.

00:39:38.437 --> 00:39:40.596
But you can imagine trying this on slightly

00:39:40.597 --> 00:39:42.726
more complicated settings and this is precisely what

00:39:42.727 --> 00:39:44.987
these people out of Stanford did. And they observed

00:39:45.047 --> 00:39:47.117
that for a fairly large number of

00:39:47.117 --> 00:39:49.277
language models, and on both

00:39:49.277 --> 00:39:51.437
kind of math and code tasks, which are particularly

00:39:51.437 --> 00:39:53.897
convenient, because it's kind of obvious what these verifiable

00:39:54.727 --> 00:39:56.967
rewards should be, they found that, in fact, when you

00:39:56.967 --> 00:39:59.217
kind of scale up the inference time compute,

00:39:59.457 --> 00:40:01.537
in the context of this talk, we're gonna be

00:40:01.537 --> 00:40:03.647
talking about inference time compute in terms of kind of

00:40:03.647 --> 00:40:05.667
parallel scaling, the number of generations

00:40:05.727 --> 00:40:07.816
per prompt, what you get is you

00:40:07.817 --> 00:40:10.077
get kind of substantially increased performance.

00:40:10.786 --> 00:40:12.816
The y axis here is the accuracy.

00:40:13.447 --> 00:40:15.277
When a language model is given kind of

00:40:15.597 --> 00:40:17.457
k, where k is on the x axis,

00:40:18.247 --> 00:40:20.507
generations per prompt, what is the probability over

00:40:20.727 --> 00:40:22.757
prompts that we're correct? At least one of those

00:40:22.757 --> 00:40:23.757
generations is correct.

00:40:25.117 --> 00:40:27.226
Now, this, paper was from two years

00:40:27.227 --> 00:40:29.707
ago, and it was fairly influential at its time.

