00:53:23.027 --> 00:53:25.276
n big, this is very small, and what really

00:53:25.276 --> 00:53:27.617
matters is the second term. And now

00:53:27.997 --> 00:53:30.317
as we increase n, we're increasing n times

00:53:30.317 --> 00:53:32.557
epsilon squared, which means our regret is getting

00:53:32.557 --> 00:53:34.277
bigger. And this leads to

00:53:34.837 --> 00:53:37.127
decreased performance, which is exactly what's going on

00:53:37.127 --> 00:53:37.257
here.

00:53:40.217 --> 00:53:42.357
So a few more remarks

00:53:42.357 --> 00:53:43.737
about this, result.

00:53:44.617 --> 00:53:46.997
So first is that with kind of optimally

00:53:47.057 --> 00:53:49.137
tuned n, right, we can never actually do this in

00:53:49.137 --> 00:53:51.157
practice. We don't know what coverage is. We don't know

00:53:51.377 --> 00:53:53.387
what epsilon squared is. But we might ask, what is the

00:53:53.387 --> 00:53:55.517
ceiling of performance for best of ben?

00:53:55.997 --> 00:53:58.177
And the serum actually gives you some scaling.

00:53:58.877 --> 00:54:00.997
So it's the coverage coefficient to the one

00:54:00.997 --> 00:54:02.717
third times epsilon to the two third.

00:54:03.566 --> 00:54:05.447
Now, kind of bound doesn't

00:54:05.747 --> 00:54:07.826
really mean anything in the abstract, but we'll

00:54:07.827 --> 00:54:09.877
compare it to an alternative method. In

00:54:09.877 --> 00:54:11.096
the next section of the talk.

00:54:12.457 --> 00:54:14.316
So really, the question here is

00:54:14.506 --> 00:54:16.587
we've done a pretty good job of analyzing best of

00:54:16.587 --> 00:54:18.926
n. Right? We kind of understand how this works.

00:54:19.007 --> 00:54:21.327
We understand how this translates into practice. But

00:54:21.327 --> 00:54:23.477
it's natural to ask if we can actually do better.

00:54:23.637 --> 00:54:25.877
Are there any kind of strategies that we can use to,

00:54:25.877 --> 00:54:27.977
