00:54:30.316 --> 00:54:31.787
annoying lack of monochromicity.

00:54:32.747 --> 00:54:34.727
And it turns out that the answer is yes.

00:54:34.967 --> 00:54:37.467
So we can instantiate something called pessimism,

00:54:37.967 --> 00:54:40.126
talk a little bit more about the etymology of pessimism

00:54:40.127 --> 00:54:42.177
in the third part of the talk. But we can

00:54:42.177 --> 00:54:44.257
instantiate pessimism, and this

00:54:44.257 --> 00:54:46.547
will kind of completely mitigate this reward hacking.

00:54:47.007 --> 00:54:49.167
Phenomenon. So

00:54:49.387 --> 00:54:51.627
what is really going on for the ceiling of performance?

00:54:52.026 --> 00:54:54.526
Why are we not getting some very good ceiling of

00:54:54.667 --> 00:54:56.727
performance? Well, in some sense, we're asking this

00:54:56.727 --> 00:54:58.637
parameter n to do double duty.

00:54:59.127 --> 00:55:01.377
Right? First, it kind of appears

00:55:01.377 --> 00:55:03.617
in this first term, and second, it appears

00:55:03.617 --> 00:55:05.897
in this second term. Right?

00:55:05.897 --> 00:55:08.046
And order to kind of better

00:55:08.046 --> 00:55:10.187
understand how we can improve on best of n, we

00:55:10.267 --> 00:55:12.507
we can ask, you know, what is best of

00:55:12.507 --> 00:55:14.606
n really doing? So it's trying

00:55:14.607 --> 00:55:16.867
to optimize this r hat, this estimated

00:55:17.006 --> 00:55:19.477
reward, but it's subject to some kind of regularization.

00:55:21.057 --> 00:55:22.657
So one other way to write

00:55:23.296 --> 00:55:25.446
this best event policy is as a solution to

00:55:25.447 --> 00:55:27.626
this variational problem. So

00:55:27.627 --> 00:55:29.566
it's essentially the policy that maximizes

00:55:29.706 --> 00:55:31.407
