00:56:51.267 --> 00:56:53.557
So let's get to the first one. Well,

00:56:53.557 --> 00:56:55.897
remember, all of this is really about solving,

00:56:56.357 --> 00:56:58.467
Goodhart's Law. When a metric becomes a

00:56:58.467 --> 00:57:00.097
target, it ceases to be a useful metric.

00:57:01.057 --> 00:57:03.457
The way that we can kind of quantitatively solve this problem

00:57:03.457 --> 00:57:05.617
is just to note that a special

00:57:05.617 --> 00:57:07.770
property of coverage is that

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:10.217
if we look at the squared error on some policy

00:57:10.437 --> 00:57:12.597
pie, then we can actually control it

00:57:12.597 --> 00:57:14.057
by the coverage coefficient

00:57:14.797 --> 00:57:17.297
times the squared error on this policy Piref.

00:57:17.816 --> 00:57:19.897
And we know that the squared error on the policy

00:57:19.897 --> 00:57:21.937
pyref is small. Right? So

00:57:21.937 --> 00:57:24.036
as long as coverage is not too large,

00:57:24.296 --> 00:57:26.377
then Goodhart's law is not really a problem. We

00:57:26.377 --> 00:57:27.637
cannot reward hack.

00:57:28.757 --> 00:57:30.997
And so this naturally suggests that we should

00:57:30.997 --> 00:57:33.177
use coverage as a regular regularizer.

00:57:37.597 --> 00:57:39.756
Now this leads maybe to the next question,

00:57:39.757 --> 00:57:41.907
which is you know, this is all very

00:57:41.907 --> 00:57:44.327
well to write down some variational problem.

00:57:44.597 --> 00:57:46.847
But how should we actually sample from this?

00:57:46.847 --> 00:57:48.887
Right? You know, you want some algorithm

00:57:48.947 --> 00:57:51.187
that kind of given your reference model and given

00:57:51.187 --> 00:57:53.407
your reward function, can actually produce some return.

00:57:54.547 --> 00:57:56.787
And it's really not obvious what how to do

00:57:56.787 --> 00:57:59.017
that from kind of this expression.

00:57:59.977 --> 00:58:02.397
And the answer is something called approximate rejection

00:58:02.487 --> 00:58:04.647
sampling. So rejection sampling originated back in the

00:58:04.647 --> 00:58:06.787
nineteen fifties with Von Neumann, with

00:58:06.787 --> 00:58:08.926
Yuri Polanski, we had a paper from

00:58:08.927 --> 00:58:11.157
a few years ago on kind of how to generalize this.

00:58:11.437 --> 00:58:13.067
Beyond bounded density ratios.

00:58:13.806 --> 00:58:16.306
But really the key point is that you can actually just directly

00:58:16.687 --> 00:58:18.807
solve this variation problem. I'm not going to

00:58:18.807 --> 00:58:20.967
write what the closed form solution actually is, but you

00:58:20.967 --> 00:58:22.986
do get a very simple closed form. You

00:58:22.987 --> 00:58:25.056
can just plug this into rejection sampling.

00:58:25.137 --> 00:58:26.517
You're not familiar with rejection

00:58:27.217 --> 00:58:29.317
sampling, there's a way in which you can take some

00:58:29.746 --> 00:58:31.806
target distribution and you have a proposal

00:58:31.867 --> 00:58:34.367
distribution that you can easily sample from. In our case,

00:58:34.627 --> 00:58:36.707
pyref. And your target distribution

00:58:36.707 --> 00:58:38.786
will be this pi beta. And as long as you know

00:58:38.787 --> 00:58:40.957
the density ratio, can kind of efficiently

00:58:40.957 --> 00:58:42.897
sample from this target distribution.

00:58:44.517 --> 00:58:46.597
So again, I mean, I'm happy to answer

00:58:46.597 --> 00:58:48.687
offline precisely what this form is. It's not

00:58:48.687 --> 00:58:50.937
very hard, but maybe just for the sake of simplicity,

00:58:50.937 --> 00:58:53.047
we you can believe me that in fact it is

00:58:53.047 --> 00:58:55.116
quite simple. And if you

00:58:55.117 --> 00:58:56.837
kind of look at the performance of the

00:58:57.216 --> 00:58:59.337
resulting policy, it has a slightly different form from

00:58:59.337 --> 00:59:00.397
that of best event.

00:59:01.457 --> 00:59:03.697
So now we're looking again at the, the

