01:17:27.797 --> 01:17:29.847
So hello, everyone. Thanks

01:17:29.847 --> 01:17:32.147
for coming. I'm Prashant Waltz, and

01:17:33.117 --> 01:17:35.436
yeah, I'm here today to discuss

01:17:35.497 --> 01:17:37.037
about an interesting phenomena

01:17:37.527 --> 01:17:39.897
that we have been observing for the past one year

01:17:40.377 --> 01:17:42.606
where a better pretrained model doesn't

01:17:42.607 --> 01:17:44.306
always lead to a better downstream model.

01:17:44.626 --> 01:17:47.037
And we show ways how we can mitigate this.

01:17:50.337 --> 01:17:52.667
So first off, I want to ask a question.

01:17:53.227 --> 01:17:55.297
So how do model builders decide

01:17:55.297 --> 01:17:56.927
their optimization decisions?

01:17:57.447 --> 01:17:59.507
So, generally, pretraining choices

01:17:59.646 --> 01:18:01.567
are tuned to optimize the base model.

01:18:01.807 --> 01:18:04.006
For example, learning rates or

01:18:04.006 --> 01:18:06.107
the optimizer opt or the

01:18:06.107 --> 01:18:08.356
optimizer are decided to minimize

01:18:08.357 --> 01:18:10.617
the pretraining loss. However,

01:18:11.557 --> 01:18:13.586
models are trained using a multistage pipeline.

01:18:13.747 --> 01:18:16.017
You have pre training, followed by

01:18:16.017 --> 01:18:17.957
post training, which includes fine tuning,

01:18:18.697 --> 01:18:20.637
reinforcement learning, or even quantization.

01:18:21.607 --> 01:18:23.617
So, essentially, our goal is to get

01:18:23.617 --> 01:18:25.227
a base model that is good

01:18:25.626 --> 01:18:26.987
after all these modifications.

01:18:28.267 --> 01:18:30.437
We argue that the pretraining loss

01:18:30.437 --> 01:18:32.747
alone doesn't capture the base model's

01:18:32.967 --> 01:18:35.047
ability to adapt So instead, we

01:18:35.047 --> 01:18:36.897
study this learning for getting trade off.

01:18:37.777 --> 01:18:38.597
So more precisely,

01:18:40.347 --> 01:18:42.427
we take a base model, which is pre trained on a large data, and

01:18:42.427 --> 01:18:44.217
then fine tune it on a dataset.

01:18:44.697 --> 01:18:46.797
On the x act we vary the hyperparameters

01:18:47.417 --> 01:18:49.457
and train various checkpoints.

01:18:49.777 --> 01:18:51.797
And on the x axis, we plot

01:18:52.337 --> 01:18:54.407
the, loss on the original pretraining

01:18:54.407 --> 01:18:56.497
task. And on the y axis, we plot

01:18:56.497 --> 01:18:58.237
the loss on the fine tuning task.

01:18:59.037 --> 01:19:00.997
So we can see that the

01:19:02.037 --> 01:19:04.356
model with the best fine tuning performance actually

01:19:04.356 --> 01:19:06.394
has some degradation on the

01:19:06.437 --> 01:19:08.697
pretraining task. Our goal to

01:19:08.697 --> 01:19:10.967
get similar or even better fine tuning

01:19:11.187 --> 01:19:13.287
performance and try to reduce

01:19:13.287 --> 01:19:15.477
this degradation. This is also

01:19:15.477 --> 01:19:17.017
called as catastrophic forgetting.

01:19:18.157 --> 01:19:20.557
In our work, we show that reducing the sharpness

01:19:20.557 --> 01:19:22.686
of the pretraining loss can actually improve

01:19:22.686 --> 01:19:24.257
this learning for getting trade off.

01:19:25.297 --> 01:19:27.747
And yeah, we evaluate

01:19:27.887 --> 01:19:30.206
three changes to the optimizer to minimize

01:19:30.207 --> 01:19:32.346
the sharp First

01:19:32.347 --> 01:19:34.606
off, it's, via explicit minimization.

01:19:35.297 --> 01:19:37.377
We use sharpness aware minimization or

01:19:37.377 --> 01:19:39.707
the SAM optimizer. SAM essentially

01:19:39.707 --> 01:19:41.787
minimizes the sensitivity to

01:19:41.787 --> 01:19:44.277
nearby perturbations. So

01:19:45.327 --> 01:19:47.367
like, fine tuning can be thought of as a perturbation

01:19:47.367 --> 01:19:49.592
where we shift the base model parameters

01:19:49.837 --> 01:19:52.306
Hence, we try Sam. And implicitly,

01:19:52.307 --> 01:19:54.467
we look at learning rates where

01:19:54.467 --> 01:19:56.626
we control the peak learning rates and also

