Okay. So let's, get right in

02:54:38.858 --> 02:54:40.238
into the setup of creativity.

02:54:41.778 --> 02:54:44.178
So there's a lot of debate on creativity.

02:54:44.738 --> 02:54:46.897
So there are some papers that are

02:54:46.897 --> 02:54:49.058
optimistic about the potential of our current

02:54:49.058 --> 02:54:51.158
systems being able to do creative things,

02:54:51.497 --> 02:54:53.658
And there's also papers on the other side

02:54:53.658 --> 02:54:55.738
that are more pessimistic claiming

02:54:55.738 --> 02:54:57.897
that a lot of current systems are actually

02:54:57.898 --> 02:54:59.948
just outputting things in the training data

02:54:59.948 --> 02:55:02.198
or are not meaningfully creative and so on.

02:55:02.838 --> 02:55:05.184
And the reason for this debate is, in a natural

02:55:05.184 --> 02:55:07.168
because it's very hard to say something

02:55:07.487 --> 02:55:09.647
about what's happening with the real systems because we don't

02:55:09.647 --> 02:55:11.718
even know what training data goes into these

02:55:11.718 --> 02:55:14.138
models, and it's hard to define novelty

02:55:14.278 --> 02:55:14.858
and creativity.

02:55:17.008 --> 02:55:19.087
So what I'll do in this talk and what we do

02:55:19.088 --> 02:55:21.117
in our paper is instead to

02:55:21.117 --> 02:55:23.198
construct minimal open

02:55:23.198 --> 02:55:24.887
ended algorithmic task,

02:55:26.088 --> 02:55:28.268
by drawing inspiration from how cognitive

02:55:28.648 --> 02:55:30.377
scientists think about creativity.

02:55:31.078 --> 02:55:33.578
And using these tasks, we will quantify

02:55:33.798 --> 02:55:35.497
some limits of our current approaches

02:55:35.888 --> 02:55:38.068
and also highlight alternatives

02:55:38.068 --> 02:55:40.288
that can do better. So that's

02:55:40.288 --> 02:55:41.358
gonna be our setup.

02:55:43.718 --> 02:55:45.978
So as an example, consider this creative

02:55:46.678 --> 02:55:47.978
leap like wordplay.

02:55:48.928 --> 02:55:51.088
A clown held the door open for me.

02:55:51.088 --> 02:55:52.308
What a nice gesture.

02:55:53.668 --> 02:55:55.988
So this work this joke works

02:55:55.988 --> 02:55:58.448
by finding a path through

02:55:58.668 --> 02:56:00.527
seemingly unconnected concepts like

02:56:01.088 --> 02:56:03.247
clown and held the door. So clown connects to

02:56:03.248 --> 02:56:05.308
gesture. Hold the door connects

02:56:05.308 --> 02:56:07.627
to a gesture, and, the joke lands

02:56:07.628 --> 02:56:09.767
because those two sound like each other.

02:56:11.428 --> 02:56:13.668
So, abstractly, we can think of that kind of

02:56:13.668 --> 02:56:15.918
creativity as finding

02:56:15.918 --> 02:56:17.387
two nodes in a concept graph

02:56:18.588 --> 02:56:20.967
and then revealing the path between them.

02:56:23.838 --> 02:56:26.338
And this is what cognitive scientists like Boden

02:56:26.948 --> 02:56:29.107
have termed combinational creativity, and you

02:56:29.108 --> 02:56:30.968
can see this is finding new combinations

02:56:31.328 --> 02:56:32.508
within the graph.

02:56:34.188 --> 02:56:36.668
So here's a one toy example that we construct

02:56:36.668 --> 02:56:38.898
that is motivated by combinational

02:56:39.358 --> 02:56:39.858
creativity.

02:56:41.598 --> 02:56:43.458
Here, the model has to generate

02:56:44.758 --> 02:56:46.918
sibling pairs along with the parent as a

02:56:46.918 --> 02:56:48.977
triplet. Where

02:56:48.978 --> 02:56:51.057
they are siblings and they share a parent

02:56:51.058 --> 02:56:53.177
in a large implicit graph that

02:56:53.178 --> 02:56:55.337
is taught to the model and the model stores

02:56:55.338 --> 02:56:57.487
this graph in the weights. So

02:56:57.568 --> 02:56:59.808
So in this graph that I have here, g

02:56:59.808 --> 02:57:02.158
and f are siblings, because they share

02:57:02.158 --> 02:57:03.518
a common parent y.

02:57:06.008 --> 02:57:08.188
And we have, like, an implicit bipartite

02:57:08.248 --> 02:57:10.488
graph that has some parents, and each parent has a

02:57:10.488 --> 02:57:11.367
lot children.

02:57:13.098 --> 02:57:15.438
So what we do is we can get training

02:57:15.658 --> 02:57:18.047
data of the following kind, like two siblings

02:57:18.048 --> 02:57:20.548
and their parent. We have a bunch of such triplets

02:57:21.308 --> 02:57:23.387
train a language model on this training

02:57:23.387 --> 02:57:25.488
data. And the question is, can

02:57:25.488 --> 02:57:27.348
the model generate new

02:57:27.728 --> 02:57:29.353
and valid new triplets

02:57:30.398 --> 02:57:32.578
from the same implicit graph. So that's

02:57:32.578 --> 02:57:34.278
what we call creativity because it's

02:57:34.758 --> 02:57:36.838
finding kind of what we are looking for, the structure,

02:57:36.838 --> 02:57:38.208
but generating new things.

02:57:39.248 --> 02:57:41.568
And we have to set up the problem carefully

02:57:41.568 --> 02:57:43.647
such that there are enough training data

02:57:43.647 --> 02:57:45.728
for the model to infer all the

02:57:45.728 --> 02:57:47.678
edges but there are

02:57:47.818 --> 02:57:50.057
also unseen triplets, that the model

02:57:50.058 --> 02:57:52.147
could generate that are valid that are

02:57:52.147 --> 02:57:54.198
not in the training data. So we

02:57:54.198 --> 02:57:55.477
ensure this is the case.

02:57:56.883 --> 02:57:58.888
So this is one example that we call

02:57:58.888 --> 02:58:00.188
the sibling discovery.

02:58:01.418 --> 02:58:03.578
And related to that, we also have a triangle

02:58:03.578 --> 02:58:05.718
discovery, which is very similar and

02:58:05.718 --> 02:58:07.798
there's an implicit graph, and the model has

02:58:07.798 --> 02:58:09.418
to generate triangles

02:58:09.898 --> 02:58:12.058
valid triangles from that graph. There

02:58:12.058 --> 02:58:14.308
is some training data and the model has to

02:58:14.308 --> 02:58:15.328
generate new triangles.

02:58:18.088 --> 02:58:19.788
There's a different kind of creativity

02:58:20.408 --> 02:58:22.508
which is called exploratory creativity,

02:58:23.788 --> 02:58:25.868
where, for example, consider the act

02:58:25.868 --> 02:58:28.017
of designing problems or molecules

02:58:28.017 --> 02:58:29.718
or deriving

02:58:30.068 --> 02:58:32.328
100 different collateries within a paradigm

02:58:32.388 --> 02:58:34.328
with some small set of laws.

02:58:35.038 --> 02:58:37.218
So here, we're not discovering preexisting

02:58:37.358 --> 02:58:39.517
connections. Instead, we want

02:58:39.517 --> 02:58:41.377
the model to generate altogether

02:58:41.678 --> 02:58:43.908
new things, subject to a few rules.

02:58:45.668 --> 02:58:47.678
And the way we model this abstractly

02:58:47.678 --> 02:58:49.738
is we ask the

02:58:49.738 --> 02:58:51.598
model to generate an adjacency

02:58:51.897 --> 02:58:53.928
list, so list of edges. And

02:58:53.928 --> 02:58:56.348
the requirement is that this list should secretly

02:58:56.808 --> 02:58:58.888
rearrange or has a permutation,

02:58:59.508 --> 02:59:01.887
to form a structure. So the structure

02:59:01.888 --> 02:59:04.068
could be something like a circle or a line

02:59:04.328 --> 02:59:06.487
could have different things, but these are the two that we

02:59:06.487 --> 02:59:08.796
consider. And we want

02:59:08.797 --> 02:59:10.968
something novel, which means that there should be a novel permutation

02:59:10.968 --> 02:59:13.378
that rearranges to form the structure.

02:59:14.188 --> 02:59:16.267
And so this is meant to mimic how in,

02:59:16.268 --> 02:59:18.128
say, a math problem or a story,

02:59:18.508 --> 02:59:20.667
we might set up many different objects

02:59:20.668 --> 02:59:23.088
in conflict, and they have to undergo

02:59:23.178 --> 02:59:25.278
a novel dynamic towards a

02:59:25.418 --> 02:59:27.568
resolution. K. So these

02:59:27.568 --> 02:59:29.508
are the two tasks that we consider

02:59:30.328 --> 02:59:32.488
and, we show some training data

02:59:32.488 --> 02:59:34.557
from that task. And we

02:59:34.557 --> 02:59:37.057
train a language model. And then we sample

02:59:37.277 --> 02:59:39.297
some n

02:59:39.298 --> 02:59:41.138
different independent generations from the model.

02:59:42.337 --> 02:59:44.598
And we measure creativity concretely

02:59:45.208 --> 02:59:47.368
quantitatively as follows. What

02:59:47.368 --> 02:59:49.798
fraction of generations are coherent,

02:59:50.098 --> 02:59:52.118
which means that, for example, in the triplet

02:59:52.178 --> 02:59:54.187
task, the two siblings that

02:59:54.188 --> 02:59:56.588
are shown and the parent, there should actually be siblings

02:59:56.588 --> 02:59:58.637
sharing that parent. They

02:59:58.638 --> 03:00:00.717
should be unseen so that we don't want things from

03:00:00.718 --> 03:00:02.948
the training data. We want new combinations,

03:00:03.008 --> 03:00:05.117
new explorations. And of course,

03:00:05.118 --> 03:00:07.598
we want them to be unique across the different samples

03:00:07.598 --> 03:00:09.738
from the model. So if we have t different

03:00:09.738 --> 03:00:11.818
samples, we wanna see what fraction of those end

03:00:11.818 --> 03:00:13.953
up being unique, unseen, and

03:00:13.953 --> 03:00:15.318
coherent. That is our creativity.

03:00:16.828 --> 03:00:19.068
K. So that's the setup.

03:00:19.068 --> 03:00:21.327
And now we can go into how

03:00:21.327 --> 03:00:23.647
well the current systems do on these simple tasks

03:00:23.648 --> 03:00:26.047
that we created where we can measure creativity

03:00:26.188 --> 03:00:27.007
very clearly.

03:00:29.008 --> 03:00:31.248
So the predominant paradigm, the standard

03:00:31.248 --> 03:00:33.407
recipe now, is autoregressive next

03:00:33.408 --> 03:00:35.568
token prediction. So where we train

03:00:35.568 --> 03:00:37.648
a model to generate one token at

03:00:37.648 --> 03:00:39.767
a time, So the model has to generate

03:00:39.827 --> 03:00:41.908
some token condition on everything that came

03:00:41.908 --> 03:00:43.358
before, which is the prefix.

03:00:44.878 --> 03:00:47.037
So the nice thing about this is it

03:00:47.037 --> 03:00:49.108
is fully general. Which

03:00:49.108 --> 03:00:50.808
means that any sequence distribution

03:00:52.558 --> 03:00:54.577
can be represented in this way on its autoregressive

03:00:55.038 --> 03:00:57.178
manner. So in

03:00:57.178 --> 03:00:59.548
principle, next token prediction

03:00:59.548 --> 03:01:00.708
should be enough.

03:01:04.268 --> 03:01:06.428
And there is sort of one,

03:01:06.668 --> 03:01:08.918
nuance about the training that we have keep

03:01:08.918 --> 03:01:10.417
in mind. So how these models are

03:01:11.297 --> 03:01:13.617
trained is at every position, the model is trained

03:01:13.618 --> 03:01:15.768
to predict the target. Given

03:01:15.768 --> 03:01:17.548
the ground truth of what came before,

03:01:18.018 --> 03:01:20.338
rather than its own generations. So our training,

03:01:20.338 --> 03:01:22.768
for example, if the model has to predict x x

03:01:22.828 --> 03:01:24.988
three, it's given x zero, x one, x two,

03:01:24.988 --> 03:01:26.948
and it has to learn to predict x three,

03:01:27.428 --> 03:01:29.528
given the ground truth. So this is often called

03:01:29.668 --> 03:01:32.018
teacher forcing because the teachers'

03:01:32.018 --> 03:01:34.178
generations of ground truth are given to the model at

03:01:34.178 --> 03:01:34.998
every position.

03:01:37.128 --> 03:01:39.207
This is a standard recipe by which models are

03:01:39.208 --> 03:01:41.257
trained. Should seem like next token

03:01:41.258 --> 03:01:43.337
prediction can more represent everything. This

03:01:43.337 --> 03:01:45.558
should work. But what

03:01:45.558 --> 03:01:48.038
we see that is perhaps surprising is

03:01:48.038 --> 03:01:49.788
that on all of these tasks,

03:01:50.348 --> 03:01:52.288
models trained in this way exhibit

03:01:52.428 --> 03:01:53.248
low creativity.

03:01:54.648 --> 03:01:57.047
So they're not able to generate

03:01:57.048 --> 03:01:59.218
new novel coherent and

03:01:59.518 --> 03:02:01.728
unseen examples with the structure that we had.

03:02:02.768 --> 03:02:05.008
And if we look into it a little bit deeper, it seems

03:02:05.008 --> 03:02:07.188
in particular that they have high memorization.

03:02:07.738 --> 03:02:09.818
Which means that whatever the

03:02:09.818 --> 03:02:11.478
model generates ends up being things

03:02:12.518 --> 03:02:14.698
from the training data itself rather than new things.

03:02:17.348 --> 03:02:19.618
So why is it that next token learning

03:02:19.938 --> 03:02:22.098
which is the standard paradigm which has worked

03:02:22.098 --> 03:02:24.468
so well in various closed ended tasks,

03:02:24.468 --> 03:02:26.628
why does it fail in even these simple

03:02:26.628 --> 03:02:28.727
creativity tasks? So

03:02:28.727 --> 03:02:30.806
let's look at what the model is supposed to learn

03:02:30.807 --> 03:02:32.378
versus what it actually learns.

03:02:33.408 --> 03:02:35.488
Ideal factorization, like, the way we want the

03:02:35.488 --> 03:02:37.828
model to solve the task, is it first

03:02:37.828 --> 03:02:40.328
guesses a parent, like, randomly picks a parent,

03:02:40.358 --> 03:02:42.777
and then generates the two siblings.

03:02:43.308 --> 03:02:45.468
So the stochastic random

03:02:45.468 --> 03:02:47.558
planning step that we want is the model to pick

03:02:47.558 --> 03:02:49.717
the parent first and then generate the two

03:02:49.718 --> 03:02:51.578
siblings and then reorder them.

03:02:52.798 --> 03:02:55.037
But if you look at the supervision from next

03:02:55.038 --> 03:02:57.287
token to, learning, because

03:02:57.287 --> 03:02:59.527
of the fact that the teacher force like, the model

03:02:59.527 --> 03:03:01.568
is given the ground truth predictions, what

03:03:01.568 --> 03:03:03.664
the model actually has learned

03:03:03.664 --> 03:03:05.438
is the three terms. So one is

03:03:06.077 --> 03:03:08.418
it has to learn probability of generating

03:03:08.478 --> 03:03:10.527
a sibling, The other one

03:03:10.587 --> 03:03:12.668
is, of, yeah, one child and then the

03:03:12.668 --> 03:03:14.967
sibling of the child. And then the last token

03:03:14.968 --> 03:03:17.448
is probability of predicting the parent given

03:03:17.448 --> 03:03:18.348
the two siblings.

03:03:21.048 --> 03:03:23.148
So the crucial thing here to note

03:03:23.577 --> 03:03:25.738
is that the last token, which is where

03:03:25.738 --> 03:03:27.438
the parent comes into the picture,

03:03:28.408 --> 03:03:30.838
there, the model has to predict the parent given

03:03:30.895 --> 03:03:33.208
the two children. And

03:03:33.208 --> 03:03:35.468
that is something that is trivial for the model to do.

03:03:35.628 --> 03:03:38.028
Because it just has to look up the parent

03:03:38.028 --> 03:03:39.408
of any one of the children

03:03:40.148 --> 03:03:42.388
and that and especially in our bike part at

03:03:42.388 --> 03:03:44.478
construction, this can be very easy to do.

03:03:44.718 --> 03:03:46.798
And so the model learns this instantly.

03:03:46.798 --> 03:03:48.208
It's a trivial shortcut.

03:03:49.068 --> 03:03:51.228
And people are probably familiar with

03:03:51.228 --> 03:03:53.347
things like simplicity bias, which is that the

03:03:53.348 --> 03:03:55.668
models will learn the shortcuts quickly if something

03:03:55.668 --> 03:03:57.928
exists. So because

03:03:57.928 --> 03:04:00.087
it's a trivial shortcut to predict the

03:04:00.088 --> 03:04:01.778
last token, which is the parent,

03:04:02.587 --> 03:04:05.067
Effectively, what's happening is the model

03:04:05.067 --> 03:04:07.458
fits that very easily, very quickly,

03:04:08.398 --> 03:04:10.478
and it's almost as if that token

03:04:10.478 --> 03:04:12.918
doesn't exist at all or we don't really get supervision

03:04:12.918 --> 03:04:14.988
from that. And the model is

03:04:14.988 --> 03:04:17.118
left to learn that

03:04:17.118 --> 03:04:19.278
these two terms, which is, you know,

03:04:19.278 --> 03:04:20.978
one child and then the sibling,

03:04:21.628 --> 03:04:23.488
which is a very hard distribution to

03:04:23.708 --> 03:04:25.578
learn without information that

03:04:25.818 --> 03:04:28.078
there is a parent that is connecting these two children.

03:04:28.718 --> 03:04:31.038
So without enough signal, the model

03:04:31.038 --> 03:04:32.897
falls back to just memorizing the

03:04:33.138 --> 03:04:35.148
training data because this is a hard

03:04:35.148 --> 03:04:35.648
distribution.

03:04:37.158 --> 03:04:39.017
So what has happened is this

03:04:39.568 --> 03:04:41.648
the fact that the parent is present because of this

03:04:41.648 --> 03:04:43.728
teacher forcing setup ends up being

03:04:44.048 --> 03:04:46.208
a shortcut for the model because it's very easy

03:04:46.208 --> 03:04:48.468
to predict the parent given the two children.

03:04:48.797 --> 03:04:50.878
And so the model doesn't really learn to use

03:04:50.878 --> 03:04:52.738
the parent and ends up memorizing

03:04:53.618 --> 03:04:55.708
the the the the two siblings.

03:04:57.788 --> 03:05:00.108
This is our intuition for why next

03:05:00.108 --> 03:05:02.487
token learning can fail in these

03:05:02.948 --> 03:05:05.068
creative tasks. And maybe you're thinking we

03:05:05.068 --> 03:05:07.028
can just reorder the tokens. What if we

03:05:07.268 --> 03:05:09.428
know, just present the training data with the parent

03:05:09.428 --> 03:05:11.608
first? In this particular case,

03:05:11.608 --> 03:05:13.368
reordering would fix the problem.

03:05:13.767 --> 03:05:15.927
But if you'll remember the triangle example that

03:05:15.928 --> 03:05:18.278
we had, there there is no

03:05:18.577 --> 03:05:20.737
easy there is no reordering that will fix the

03:05:20.738 --> 03:05:22.748
problem. Because given any two

03:05:22.748 --> 03:05:24.928
tokens, it's easy to predict the third token.

03:05:25.527 --> 03:05:27.868
Of the triangle or the third edge of the triangle.

03:05:28.168 --> 03:05:30.358
And so any ordering will have the same

03:05:30.358 --> 03:05:32.448
problem with the intuition that, like, once you

03:05:32.448 --> 03:05:34.678
have the first few objects,

03:05:34.678 --> 03:05:36.838
it becomes really easy to predict the, like,

03:05:36.838 --> 03:05:39.018
the final one, and so the model doesn't learn

03:05:39.078 --> 03:05:40.538
the overall big structure.

03:05:43.287 --> 03:05:45.447
So if this is true, we can try to remove

03:05:45.448 --> 03:05:47.307
the shortcut and see what happens.

03:05:48.128 --> 03:05:50.228
And one way to remove the shortcut

03:05:50.398 --> 03:05:52.658
is that, at least in a few samples, we

03:05:52.798 --> 03:05:54.578
can remove the teacher forcing,

03:05:54.838 --> 03:05:57.078
So the model has to predict the for example,

03:05:57.078 --> 03:05:59.278
has to predict the entire triplet at once,

03:05:59.438 --> 03:06:01.858
It's not predicting the parent given the children.

03:06:02.108 --> 03:06:03.807
And so we've removed that shortcut.

03:06:04.228 --> 03:06:06.328
And one way to do this is called teacher

03:06:06.388 --> 03:06:08.526
less training. Where we can just give some math

03:06:08.527 --> 03:06:10.667
tokens or some trivial tokens as a

03:06:10.668 --> 03:06:13.087
prefix and get the model to predict all of them.

03:06:15.068 --> 03:06:17.228
So we usually train a model on a mixture of

03:06:17.228 --> 03:06:19.316
the two so that the model knows how to

03:06:19.316 --> 03:06:21.348
use its own generations at test time, but

03:06:21.348 --> 03:06:23.408
then we've also removed the shortcut on a few

03:06:23.408 --> 03:06:25.648
examples by getting the model to predict all the

03:06:25.648 --> 03:06:26.457
tokens at once.

03:06:28.458 --> 03:06:30.538
And we see that across all of our

03:06:30.538 --> 03:06:32.868
tasks. This kind of teacher less

03:06:32.868 --> 03:06:34.838
training or multi token prediction

03:06:35.878 --> 03:06:38.008
does end up being more creative than next

03:06:38.328 --> 03:06:40.528
token learning, which is the standard paradigm.

03:06:41.538 --> 03:06:43.238
So this shortcut that we identified

03:06:43.778 --> 03:06:45.768
earlier seems to be at least one of

03:06:46.248 --> 03:06:48.328
factors contributing to poor creativity of

03:06:48.328 --> 03:06:50.407
next token learning because when we remove

03:06:50.408 --> 03:06:52.268
that shortcut, we see that the creativity

03:06:52.568 --> 03:06:53.458
does go up.

03:06:55.378 --> 03:06:57.538
So that gets me to the first, sort of

03:06:57.538 --> 03:06:59.878
takeaway. I can't believe that next

03:06:59.878 --> 03:07:02.117
token learning, which seems to work really well

03:07:02.118 --> 03:07:04.287
on tasks where the goal is very clear,

03:07:04.448 --> 03:07:06.768
On many tasks, it works very well, including many

03:07:06.768 --> 03:07:08.908
complex real world setups. But

03:07:08.908 --> 03:07:10.988
even in these simple tasks where you

03:07:10.988 --> 03:07:13.148
have this latent structure, the model

03:07:13.288 --> 03:07:15.448
seems to fail. This learning paradigm seems

03:07:15.448 --> 03:07:16.088
to fail.

03:07:17.528 --> 03:07:20.028
So just to summarize that a little more abstractly,

03:07:20.648 --> 03:07:22.828
in general, in a creative process,

03:07:22.848 --> 03:07:24.868
the outputs are generated

03:07:24.928 --> 03:07:26.977
after some kind of thinking, which is

03:07:27.378 --> 03:07:29.448
the stochastic planning part, and that

03:07:29.448 --> 03:07:31.487
can be unobserved. And for

03:07:31.488 --> 03:07:33.665
example, in the triangle setup, it's that you

03:07:33.665 --> 03:07:35.686
don't see the actual process by which

03:07:35.687 --> 03:07:37.708
you figure out, like, search over the

03:07:37.709 --> 03:07:39.758
triangles. And when we

03:07:39.758 --> 03:07:41.918
have such a setup, next token learning

03:07:41.918 --> 03:07:44.088
can fail because once we have

03:07:44.088 --> 03:07:46.408
the entire thing given, like, the model, it's trivial

03:07:46.408 --> 03:07:48.477
to predict certain feature

03:07:48.478 --> 03:07:50.088
forcing, and so there are these shortcuts

03:07:50.568 --> 03:07:52.808
which make next token learning bad, and it ends

03:07:52.808 --> 03:07:54.548
up memorizing the data instead.

03:07:54.948 --> 03:07:57.267
And removing these shortcuts can actually

03:07:57.268 --> 03:07:59.357
improve creativity, for example,

03:07:59.358 --> 03:08:01.618
through teacher less multi token training.

03:08:02.668 --> 03:08:04.827
In the paper, we also look at diffusion language

03:08:04.827 --> 03:08:07.098
models, which are also a version of removing

03:08:07.098 --> 03:08:09.418
the shortcut because, there again, you have to predict

03:08:09.418 --> 03:08:11.398
a few tokens given masked every

03:08:11.568 --> 03:08:13.458
some other tokens. So there is also

03:08:13.618 --> 03:08:15.948
we can think of that as removing shortcuts and that

03:08:16.158 --> 03:08:16.698
also improves here.

03:08:19.688 --> 03:08:21.768
Now there's a second issue with

03:08:21.768 --> 03:08:23.468
our current paradigm, which

03:08:24.008 --> 03:08:25.988
is that even if we, you know, are

03:08:26.388 --> 03:08:28.668
preventing memorization of the training data,

03:08:28.668 --> 03:08:30.773
what can happen and what

03:08:30.774 --> 03:08:32.308
is observed and noted is that

03:08:32.868 --> 03:08:34.738
the different generations from the model

03:08:35.028 --> 03:08:37.108
might end up being the same. So even

03:08:37.108 --> 03:08:39.267
if we generate multiple samples, we don't get

03:08:39.268 --> 03:08:39.758
variety.

03:08:41.538 --> 03:08:43.938
And you might wonder, let's just raise the temperature

03:08:43.938 --> 03:08:46.112
because that's a defect or knob, and that

03:08:46.113 --> 03:08:48.116
should fix this. But what we see

03:08:48.116 --> 03:08:50.307
is when we increase the temperature, it's not

03:08:50.307 --> 03:08:52.447
a a free lunch because the

03:08:52.448 --> 03:08:54.488
coherence also drops. And

03:08:54.488 --> 03:08:56.577
the temperature sampling is just adding some entropy

03:08:56.577 --> 03:08:59.028
when we sample the output tokens, and so

03:08:59.028 --> 03:09:00.888
the model overall becomes less coherent.

03:09:02.628 --> 03:09:04.868
So here's, our intuition, for

03:09:04.868 --> 03:09:07.188
thinking about this. In a transformer,

03:09:07.328 --> 03:09:09.408
diversity is typically elicited through

03:09:09.408 --> 03:09:11.428
temperature sampling and this might

03:09:11.428 --> 03:09:13.448
be suboptimal because in this

03:09:13.668 --> 03:09:15.968
way, the model has to actually flush out many

03:09:15.968 --> 03:09:18.188
thoughts in parallel And then

03:09:18.188 --> 03:09:20.268
while we are sampling the tokens, it kind

03:09:20.268 --> 03:09:22.178
of has to end up picking one or the other.

03:09:22.658 --> 03:09:24.918
And so this seems like much hard to do.

03:09:25.138 --> 03:09:27.028
A more natural way and maybe

03:09:27.348 --> 03:09:28.958
how we would approach this is

03:09:29.438 --> 03:09:31.518
think about many ideas and then you pick one of

03:09:31.518 --> 03:09:33.738
them. And then you flush that out

03:09:33.738 --> 03:09:34.668
one thought at a time.

03:09:37.327 --> 03:09:39.507
And so we can think of bringing the randomness

03:09:39.648 --> 03:09:41.848
to the input and instead, but the model can, like, think

03:09:41.848 --> 03:09:43.928
about different things and pick one idea and then

03:09:43.928 --> 03:09:44.598
commit to it.

03:09:45.958 --> 03:09:48.158
So what this concretely means is

03:09:48.158 --> 03:09:50.398
in standard training, we go from some prefix

03:09:50.398 --> 03:09:52.337
or a prompt to the generations directly.

03:09:53.358 --> 03:09:55.517
Instead, what we'll do is some seed conditioning, which

03:09:55.517 --> 03:09:57.527
is you have your prompt and

03:09:57.527 --> 03:09:59.787
then you also give a random string to

03:09:59.928 --> 03:10:02.108
the model. And the hope is the model can use

03:10:02.108 --> 03:10:04.128
this random string, the prefix

03:10:04.418 --> 03:10:06.577
as the source of randomness to generate

03:10:06.578 --> 03:10:08.388
different things, after that.

03:10:09.188 --> 03:10:11.428
And so the way we operationalize this is

03:10:11.428 --> 03:10:13.358
every training data is given, like,

03:10:13.918 --> 03:10:15.996
some random string that is fixed, and we

03:10:15.997 --> 03:10:18.327
train the model with these random strings.

03:10:18.488 --> 03:10:20.748
And at that time, we give new random strings,

03:10:20.808 --> 03:10:22.908
and and that by changing the randomness

03:10:23.288 --> 03:10:25.558
in the string at time, we get new samples.

03:10:26.698 --> 03:10:28.858
So different seeds can lead to different outputs

03:10:28.858 --> 03:10:30.848
or different samples. And

03:10:30.988 --> 03:10:33.168
maybe this can prevent the drop in coherence

03:10:33.468 --> 03:10:35.708
because now the model can actually focus

03:10:35.708 --> 03:10:37.728
on one thought at a time rather than

03:10:37.728 --> 03:10:39.968
what happens when we increase the temperature where we

03:10:39.968 --> 03:10:42.248
just kind of adding a lot of entropy to the

03:10:42.248 --> 03:10:42.908
output distribution.

03:10:45.307 --> 03:10:47.628
So we call this naive seed conditioning

03:10:47.628 --> 03:10:49.898
because we're literally prepending a completely

03:10:49.898 --> 03:10:51.978
random string to each training example. So

03:10:51.978 --> 03:10:54.158
there's no structure in how we get these strings.

03:10:56.208 --> 03:10:58.708
Is really surprising is that despite the simplicity,

03:10:59.628 --> 03:11:01.787
even with the, with zero

03:11:01.787 --> 03:11:04.287
temperature, which means you've turned off any randomness

03:11:04.517 --> 03:11:06.458
from the output, and all the randomness

03:11:06.598 --> 03:11:09.037
comes from these strings. We see that seed conditioning

03:11:09.038 --> 03:11:10.337
is actually comparable

03:11:11.178 --> 03:11:12.908
temperature sampling in creativity.

03:11:13.388 --> 03:11:15.546
So the model is somehow able to use these

03:11:15.547 --> 03:11:17.698
random strings of prefixes and

03:11:17.698 --> 03:11:20.118
convert them to a meaningful source of randomness

03:11:20.258 --> 03:11:22.398
that leads to more

03:11:22.398 --> 03:11:24.478
diverse samples that also have the

03:11:24.478 --> 03:11:26.228
structure. That we want.

03:11:27.268 --> 03:11:29.448
So this shows that this kind of seed conditioning

03:11:29.508 --> 03:11:31.767
could be a reasonable order alternative

03:11:31.988 --> 03:11:34.067
for diversity elicitation compared to the

03:11:34.067 --> 03:11:36.238
de facto standard of temperature sum. Sampling.

03:11:37.878 --> 03:11:40.298
What's also really cool is in some cases,

03:11:40.598 --> 03:11:42.608
seed conditioning ends up being

03:11:42.827 --> 03:11:45.067
the most creative method, outperforming the

03:11:45.067 --> 03:11:46.648
best temperature as well.

03:11:48.218 --> 03:11:50.458
So this intuition that, fleshing

03:11:50.458 --> 03:11:52.598
out many thoughts at a time might be suboptimal

03:11:52.658 --> 03:11:54.698
and instead we should flush out one thought

03:11:54.698 --> 03:11:56.738
at a time by bringing the randomness to the

03:11:56.738 --> 03:11:58.897
input seems to hold for these

03:11:58.898 --> 03:12:00.087
exam that we saw.

03:12:02.317 --> 03:12:04.638
So that takes me to the second, surprise.

03:12:04.638 --> 03:12:06.718
We all use temperature sampling currently as

03:12:06.718 --> 03:12:08.958
a way of getting diversity, but temperature

03:12:08.958 --> 03:12:10.578
sampling is actually not as good.

03:12:11.138 --> 03:12:13.298
And even something very simple, like adding

03:12:13.298 --> 03:12:15.348
random strings into the

03:12:15.348 --> 03:12:17.587
prefix seemed to improve the diversity

03:12:17.588 --> 03:12:18.578
of the models generated.

03:12:22.748 --> 03:12:24.827
So I've shown two different bottlenecks

03:12:24.827 --> 03:12:26.918
in current paradigms, and there are a lot of you know,

03:12:26.918 --> 03:12:29.077
nice ideas that we could scale up.

03:12:29.077 --> 03:12:31.188
And we've started doing some of in the group

03:12:31.188 --> 03:12:33.168
now. And I'll talk about one particular

03:12:33.228 --> 03:12:35.237
thing that we did on larger

03:12:35.777 --> 03:12:38.077
scale mathematical reasoning. So it's

03:12:38.077 --> 03:12:40.178
a what we do is, like, a close cousin

03:12:40.238 --> 03:12:42.458
of seed conditioning, which can improve

03:12:42.458 --> 03:12:42.868
performance.

03:12:44.848 --> 03:12:46.868
So this is a paper called Mode Conditioning.

03:12:47.568 --> 03:12:49.698
At this, I clear that was presented.

03:12:51.918 --> 03:12:54.158
So the standard paradigm that's very popular

03:12:54.158 --> 03:12:56.188
now with reasoning is what

03:12:56.188 --> 03:12:58.428
is called test time scaling. And so one

03:12:58.428 --> 03:13:00.568
way to do this is to sample

03:13:00.948 --> 03:13:02.888
multiple traces from the model independently

03:13:03.538 --> 03:13:05.618
And then if you have a good verifier, which is

03:13:05.618 --> 03:13:07.558
the case for a lot of these math settings,

03:13:07.878 --> 03:13:09.958
then you can call success if any of your

03:13:09.958 --> 03:13:11.578
samples ends up being correct.

03:13:13.118 --> 03:13:15.297
So you can sample k times you have effectively

03:13:15.358 --> 03:13:16.257
k trials.

03:13:18.608 --> 03:13:20.847
But standard training can end up

03:13:20.848 --> 03:13:22.901
wasting a lot of samples because

03:13:22.901 --> 03:13:25.088
of low diversity. So the same issue as before,

03:13:25.088 --> 03:13:27.138
if all the samples end up looking the same, then

03:13:27.138 --> 03:13:29.208
this is really a waste. And so

03:13:29.208 --> 03:13:31.448
if you look at the top panel here, like, let's

03:13:31.448 --> 03:13:32.908
say a model sees both

03:13:34.268 --> 03:13:36.418
breadth for search and depth for search in the training data.

03:13:36.978 --> 03:13:39.057
In standard training, what ends up happening is the

03:13:39.057 --> 03:13:41.148
model ends up picking one of one of the

03:13:41.148 --> 03:13:43.468
two as a predominant strategy. And

03:13:43.468 --> 03:13:45.708
many of the samples end up being basically the

03:13:45.708 --> 03:13:47.918
same attempt. And so we're not meaningfully

03:13:48.058 --> 03:13:50.217
you know, utilizing the fact that we can

03:13:50.218 --> 03:13:51.208
try different things.

03:13:52.888 --> 03:13:54.968
And you're having the same intuition as before

03:13:54.968 --> 03:13:56.986
that maybe we should have the model of

03:13:56.987 --> 03:13:59.168
pickets of random strategy in the beginning and

03:13:59.168 --> 03:14:01.407
then come into that strategy, and that's a better

03:14:01.408 --> 03:14:03.318
way of getting, diversity

03:14:03.778 --> 03:14:05.938
in samples. So for example, if we

03:14:05.938 --> 03:14:07.968
knew which training samples were

03:14:07.968 --> 03:14:09.818
breadth first search or depth first search,

03:14:09.978 --> 03:14:12.057
then we can do this mode condition training

03:14:12.058 --> 03:14:13.767
where we introduce this prefix

03:14:14.328 --> 03:14:16.408
to every sample and so the model knows

03:14:16.408 --> 03:14:18.288
how to use a BFS prefix or

03:14:18.448 --> 03:14:20.527
a DFS prefix. And at test

03:14:20.528 --> 03:14:22.778
time, we can now ensure by changing the

03:14:22.778 --> 03:14:24.888
prefix manually. That's let's say

03:14:24.888 --> 03:14:26.967
50% of the samples try BFS,

03:14:26.968 --> 03:14:29.178
50% of samples try BFS.

03:14:30.218 --> 03:14:32.338
So this way, mode condition training as well

03:14:32.338 --> 03:14:33.848
as inference allow us

03:14:34.528 --> 03:14:36.848
to explicitly try all the modes at

03:14:36.848 --> 03:14:38.898
test time by changing the prefixes

03:14:38.898 --> 03:14:40.177
that we provide to the model.

03:14:41.748 --> 03:14:43.988
So with f so intuitively with

03:14:43.988 --> 03:14:46.087
the same inference budget, we can probably

03:14:46.088 --> 03:14:47.198
sample more diverse things.

03:14:49.608 --> 03:14:51.768
Of course, there's a practical question of where do we get

03:14:51.768 --> 03:14:53.898
these modes. Like, how do we know which strategy

03:14:53.898 --> 03:14:55.978
is used in which training data? So we

03:14:55.978 --> 03:14:58.308
do something very simple, and there's probably more

03:14:58.948 --> 03:15:00.808
complicated, nicer things that one can do.

03:15:01.028 --> 03:15:02.977
But we take some real world data

03:15:03.458 --> 03:15:05.478
like NuminaMac and simply cluster

03:15:05.538 --> 03:15:07.798
the gradients And then, let's

03:15:08.018 --> 03:15:10.024
say, into k clusters, and then

03:15:10.024 --> 03:15:12.168
we assign one a a label to each of

03:15:12.168 --> 03:15:14.248
these clusters as the mode labels. In

03:15:14.248 --> 03:15:16.408
the experiments I'm gonna show you, we just, you know, pick

03:15:16.408 --> 03:15:18.457
two clusters just for convenience. So we

03:15:18.458 --> 03:15:20.318
just formed two clusters from the training

03:15:20.698 --> 03:15:22.408
data automatically and there's really simple way.

03:15:23.668 --> 03:15:26.297
And we evaluate the performance on 500.

03:15:27.018 --> 03:15:29.258
And I have the results here. So we see

03:15:29.258 --> 03:15:31.317
that across you know, various model sizes.

03:15:32.248 --> 03:15:34.328
What happened is, especially for

03:15:34.328 --> 03:15:36.618
larger k, we see that mode

03:15:36.618 --> 03:15:38.398
conditioning, mod c here

03:15:38.738 --> 03:15:40.898
ends up performing much better because

03:15:40.898 --> 03:15:42.918
it's able to better use the diversity

03:15:43.058 --> 03:15:45.418
that we can get at test time. And

03:15:45.438 --> 03:15:47.658
so for the same, Passat

03:15:47.718 --> 03:15:48.028
k

03:15:51.708 --> 03:15:53.997
more fewer inference samples by

03:15:54.178 --> 03:15:56.268
simply preventing this mode collapse issue.

03:15:57.708 --> 03:15:59.867
And I just wanted to highlight that we don't use

03:15:59.868 --> 03:16:02.068
any auxiliary We're using just the same

03:16:02.068 --> 03:16:04.148
training data and automatically figuring out

03:16:04.148 --> 03:16:06.198
the modes. And this is one of those rare

03:16:06.198 --> 03:16:08.438
times where with the same data and no extra

03:16:08.438 --> 03:16:10.658
information, we're actually able to get, like, a

03:16:10.978 --> 03:16:12.998
consistent gain across many, many settings

03:16:13.057 --> 03:16:15.237
by addressing this fundamental mode collapse

03:16:15.238 --> 03:16:15.738
issue.

03:16:19.258 --> 03:16:21.188
And, we do this for

03:16:21.508 --> 03:16:23.128
but the nice thing is it's also

03:16:24.028 --> 03:16:26.128
ends up being a better starting point

03:16:26.268 --> 03:16:28.497
for reinforcement learning because these mode

03:16:28.498 --> 03:16:30.898
conditioning and doing these mode using

03:16:30.898 --> 03:16:33.218
these modes at task time, also allow

03:16:33.218 --> 03:16:34.678
for better controlled exploration.

03:16:35.518 --> 03:16:37.538
And so we see that mode conditioning

03:16:37.598 --> 03:16:39.618
SFT plus RL outperforms

03:16:40.077 --> 03:16:42.348
standard SFT plus RL across all k,

03:16:42.507 --> 03:16:44.527
And, interestingly, the usual

03:16:44.587 --> 03:16:46.598
criticism of RL or, like, the usual

03:16:46.598 --> 03:16:48.607
down is that at large scale, it

03:16:48.608 --> 03:16:50.767
ends up underperforming the base model because

03:16:50.767 --> 03:16:52.998
RL itself causes a diverse to

03:16:52.998 --> 03:16:55.076
collapse. But with mode conditioning, we're seeing

03:16:55.077 --> 03:16:57.248
that we are outperforming the base the

03:16:57.248 --> 03:16:59.218
starting point across all k, because

03:16:59.698 --> 03:17:01.878
actually preserving, the kind of diversity

03:17:02.798 --> 03:17:04.958
even through the audit process, and we're not and it's sort

03:17:04.958 --> 03:17:06.988
of mitigating the diversity collapse issue

03:17:07.768 --> 03:17:08.888
of reinforcement learning as well.

03:17:10.087 --> 03:17:12.428
And so the kind of the broader idea

03:17:12.567 --> 03:17:14.668
is that temperature sampling might not be a

03:17:14.668 --> 03:17:16.828
good idea to get meaningful diversity from the

03:17:16.828 --> 03:17:18.968
model. And if we introduce even

03:17:19.028 --> 03:17:20.998
simple things like having these ways of

03:17:21.078 --> 03:17:23.338
telling the model which modes to use and

03:17:23.398 --> 03:17:25.528
allowing the model to try different modes

03:17:25.528 --> 03:17:27.388
at test time in a controlled manner.

03:17:27.668 --> 03:17:29.988
That vastly improves the performance of these models.

03:17:33.678 --> 03:17:35.978
Okay. So I'll, sort of conclude.

03:17:36.118 --> 03:17:38.057
So the big picture is that we identified

03:17:38.358 --> 03:17:40.648
two orthogonal failures

03:17:40.708 --> 03:17:42.578
of standard paradigms for creative tasks,

03:17:42.738 --> 03:17:45.078
and these are somewhat surprising because we

03:17:45.298 --> 03:17:47.398
have converged the standard paradigm, and it feels

03:17:47.398 --> 03:17:49.467
like we can do a lot. But there are also

03:17:50.028 --> 03:17:52.128
some fundamental blind spots that we identify

03:17:52.268 --> 03:17:54.628
through our simple creative tasks.

03:17:55.428 --> 03:17:57.928
So on this next token learning versus multi token

03:17:57.988 --> 03:18:00.128
learning, because of the shortcuts,

03:18:00.268 --> 03:18:02.368
and then there's also the seed conditioning

03:18:02.508 --> 03:18:04.658
as an alternative to temperature sampling

03:18:04.718 --> 03:18:07.117
as a way of getting diversity from these models.

03:18:07.757 --> 03:18:10.157
And I think I mentioned this before. There are interesting connections

03:18:10.158 --> 03:18:12.318
to diffusion models that I did didn't get into, but

